<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-07">
  <title>Week 7: Second-order linear ODEs</title>
<subsection xml:id="second-order-introduction">
    <title>Introduction to Second-Order Differential Equations</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            After studying this section, you should be able to:
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>Define a second-order differential equation.</li>
            <li>Distinguish between linear and nonlinear differential equations.</li>
            <li>Distinguish between homogeneous and nonhomogeneous equations.</li>
            <li>Explain why exponential functions are used to solve constant-coefficient differential equations.</li>
            <li>Form the characteristic equation.</li>
            <li>Determine solutions corresponding to distinct real roots, repeated roots, and complex roots.</li>
            <li>Describe common methods used to solve nonhomogeneous equations.</li>
            <li>Apply variation of parameters to find particular solutions.</li>
        </ol>
    </introduction>

    <p>
        A second-order differential equation is an equation involving the second
        derivative of an unknown function. Many mathematical models from
        physics and engineering naturally lead to second-order differential
        equations.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-second-order">
        <title>Second-Order Differential Equation</title>

        <p>
            A second-order differential equation is an equation involving the
            second derivative of an unknown function.
        </p>

        <me>
            F(x,y,y',y'')=0
        </me>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="ex-second-order">
        <title>Examples</title>

        <p>The following are second-order differential equations.</p>

        <me>
            y''+4y'-5y=0
        </me>

        <me>
            x^2y''+xy'-3y=\sin x
        </me>

        <me>
            y''=(y')^2
        </me>
    </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="linear-second-order">
        <title>Linear Second-Order Differential Equations</title>

        <p>
            The standard form of a linear second-order differential equation is
        </p>

        <me>
            a(x)y''+b(x)y'+c(x)y=g(x)
        </me>

        <p>
            where <m>a(x)\neq 0</m>.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-homogeneous">
            <title>Homogeneous Equation</title>

            <p>
                If
                <m>g(x)=0</m>,
                then
            </p>

            <me>
                a(x)y''+b(x)y'+c(x)y=0
            </me>

            <p>
                is called a homogeneous differential equation.
            </p>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-nonhomogeneous">
            <title>Nonhomogeneous Equation</title>

            <p>
                If
                <m>g(x)\neq 0</m>,
                then the equation is called nonhomogeneous.
            </p>
        </definition>

        <example xml:id="ex-nonhomogeneous">
            <title>A Nonhomogeneous Equation</title>

            <me>
                y''-3y'+2y=e^x
            </me>

            <p>
                This is a nonhomogeneous linear differential equation.
            </p>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="constant-coefficient">
        <title>Constant-Coefficient Equations</title>

        <p>
            In this part we focus primarily on equations of the form
        </p>

        <me>
            ay''+by'+cy=0
        </me>

        <p>
            where <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> are constants.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="why-exponentials">
        <title>Why Exponential Functions?</title>

        <p>
            Exponential functions are useful because differentiation preserves
            their basic form.
        </p>

        <p>
            Suppose
        </p>

        <me>
            y=e^{\lambda x}
        </me>

        <p>
            Then
        </p>

        <me>
            y'=\lambda e^{\lambda x}
        </me>

        <me>
            y''=\lambda^2 e^{\lambda x}
        </me>

        <p>
            Substituting these expressions into
        </p>

        <me>
            ay''+by'+cy=0
        </me>

        <p>
            gives
        </p>

        <me>
            a(\lambda^2e^{\lambda x})+b(\lambda e^{\lambda x})+ce^{\lambda x}=0
        </me>

        <p>
            Factoring out <m>e^{\lambda x}</m> yields
        </p>

        <me>
            e^{\lambda x}(a\lambda^2+b\lambda+c)=0
        </me>

        <p>
            Since <m>e^{\lambda x}\neq0</m>, we obtain
        </p>

        <me>
            a\lambda^2+b\lambda+c=0
        </me>

        <p>
            This algebraic equation is called the characteristic equation.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="characteristic-roots">
        <title>Solutions from Characteristic Roots</title>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-characteristic-roots">
            <title>Characteristic Root Cases</title>

            <p>
                Consider
            </p>

            <me>
                ay''+by'+cy=0.
            </me>

            <p>
                Let
            </p>

            <me>
                a\lambda^2+b\lambda+c=0
            </me>

            <p>
                be its characteristic equation.
            </p>

            <ol>
                <li>
                    If the roots are distinct real numbers
                    <m>\lambda_1</m> and <m>\lambda_2</m>, then
                    <me>
                        y=C_1e^{\lambda_1x}+C_2e^{\lambda_2x}.
                    </me>
                </li>

                <li>
                    If the root <m>\lambda</m> is repeated, then
                    <me>
                        y=C_1e^{\lambda x}+C_2xe^{\lambda x}.
                    </me>
                </li>

                <li>
                    If the roots are
                    <m>\alpha\pm\beta i</m>, then
                    <me>
                        y=e^{\alpha x}
                        \left(
                        C_1\cos(\beta x)
                        +
                        C_2\sin(\beta x)
                        \right).
                    </me>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </theorem>
      <p>  To determine the constants <m> C_1 </m> and <m> C_2 </m>, we need two conditions, usually given as
        <me>
            y(x_0)=y_0\quad\text{and}\quad y'(x_0)=y_1.
        </me>
        </p>
        <example>
          Find the solution of
          <me>
            y''-3y'+2y=0,\quad y(0)=1,\quad y'(0)=0.
          </me>
          <solution>
            <p>
              The characteristic equation is
            </p>  
            <me> 
              \lambda^2-3\lambda+2=0.
            </me>
            <p>
              The roots are <m>\lambda_1=1</m> and <m>\lambda_2=2</m>. Thus the general solution is
            </p>
            <me>
              y=C_1e^x+C_2e^{2x}.
            </me>
            <p>
              Using the initial conditions, we have
            </p>
            <me>
              y(0)=C_1+C_2=1
            </me>
            <me>
              y'(0)=C_1+2C_2=0
            </me>
            <p>
              Solving this system of equations, we find <m>C_1=2</m> and <m>C_2=-1</m>. Therefore, the solution is
            </p>
            <me>
              y=2e^x-e^{2x}.
            </me>
          </solution>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="nonhomogeneous-methods">
        <title>Methods for Nonhomogeneous Equations</title>

        <p>
            For a nonhomogeneous equation
        </p>

        <me>
            ay''+by'+cy=g(x),
        </me>

        <p>
            the general solution has the form
        </p>

        <me>
            y=y_c+y_p,
        </me>

        <p>
            where <m>y_c</m> is the complementary solution and
            <m>y_p</m> is a particular solution.
        </p>

        <p>
            Common methods for finding particular solutions include:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>Method of Undetermined Coefficients</li>
            <li>Variation of Parameters</li>
            <li>Reduction of Order</li>
            <li>Power Series Methods</li>
        </ul>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="variation-of-parameters">
        <title>Variation of Parameters</title>

        <p>
            Variation of parameters is one of the most general methods for
            finding particular solutions of linear differential equations.
            Unlike undetermined coefficients, it may be used for variable-
            coefficient equations.
        </p>

        <p>
            Consider
        </p>

        <me>
            y''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=g(x).
        </me>

        <p>
            Suppose the corresponding homogeneous equation has linearly
            independent solutions <m>y_1(x)</m> and <m>y_2(x)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Then
        </p>

        <me>
            y_c=C_1y_1+C_2y_2.
        </me>

        <p>
            Variation of parameters replaces the constants by functions:
        </p>

        <me>
            y_p=u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x).
        </me>

        <p>
            Define the Wronskian
        </p>

        <me>
            W=\begin{vmatrix}
            y_1 &amp; y_2 \\
            y_1' &amp; y_2'
            \end{vmatrix}=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2.
        </me>

        <p>
            The functions <m>u_1</m> and <m>u_2</m> satisfy
        </p>

        <me>
            u_1'=-\frac{y_2g}{W}
        </me>

        <me>
            u_2'=\frac{y_1g}{W}
        </me>

        <p>
            Integrating gives
        </p>

        <me>
            u_1=\int -\frac{y_2g}{W}\,dx
        </me>

        <me>
            u_2=\int \frac{y_1g}{W}\,dx
        </me>

        <p>
            A particular solution is
        </p>

        <me>
            y_p=u_1y_1+u_2y_2,
        </me>

        <p>
            and the complete solution is
        </p>

        <me>
            y=y_c+y_p.
        </me>
    </subsection>

    <example xml:id="ex-vop-cauchy-euler">
        <title>Variation of Parameters for a Variable-Coefficient Equation</title>

        <p>Solve</p>

        <me>
            x^2y''-2xy'+2y=x^2,
            \qquad x>0.
        </me>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Divide by <m>x^2</m>.
            </p>

            <me>
                y''-\frac{2}{x}y'+\frac{2}{x^2}y=1.
            </me>

            <p>
                Solve the homogeneous equation by trying
                <m>y=x^{\lambda}</m>.
            </p>

            <me>
                \lambda(\lambda-1)-2\lambda+2=0
            </me>

            <me>
                \lambda^2-3\lambda+2=0
            </me>

            <me>
                (\lambda-1)(\lambda-2)=0.
            </me>

            <p>
                Thus
            </p>

            <me>
                y_1=x,
                \qquad
                y_2=x^2.
            </me>

            <p>
                The complementary solution is
            </p>

            <me>
                y_c=C_1x+C_2x^2.
            </me>

            <p>
                Compute the Wronskian.
            </p>

            <me>
                W=
                \begin{vmatrix}
                x &amp; x^2\\
                1 &amp; 2x
                \end{vmatrix}
                =x^2.
            </me>

            <p>
                Since <m>g(x)=1</m>,
            </p>

            <me>
                u_1'
                =
                -\frac{x^2}{x^2}
                =
                -1
            </me>

            <me>
                u_2'
                =
                \frac{x}{x^2}
                =
                \frac1x
            </me>

            <p>
                Integrating gives
            </p>

            <me>
                u_1=-x,
                \qquad
                u_2=\ln x.
            </me>

            <p>
                Therefore
            </p>

            <me>
                y_p
                =
                (-x)(x)+(\ln x)(x^2)
            </me>

            <me>
                y_p=-x^2+x^2\ln x.
            </me>

            <p>
                Since <m>-x^2</m> belongs to the complementary solution,
                we may absorb it into the constant <m>C_2</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Hence,
            </p>

            <me>
                y_p=x^2\ln x.
            </me>

            <p>
                The solution is
            </p>

            <me>
                y=C_1x+C_2x^2+x^2\ln x.
            </me>
        </solution>
    </example>
<subsection xml:id="reduction-of-order">
    <title>Reduction of Order</title>

    <p>
        Reduction of order is a method used to find a second linearly
        independent solution of a homogeneous second-order differential
        equation when one solution is already known.
    </p>

    <p>
        Consider the differential equation
    </p>

    <me>
        y''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0.
    </me>

    <p>
        Suppose one solution, <m>y_1(x)</m>, is known. To find a second
        solution, we assume that it has the form
    </p>

    <me>
        y_2=v(x)y_1(x),
    </me>

    <p>
        where <m>v(x)</m> is an unknown function.
    </p>

    <p>
        The procedure is:
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>Assume <m>y_2=v(x)y_1(x)</m>.</li>
        <li>Compute <m>y_2'</m> and <m>y_2''</m>.</li>
        <li>Substitute into the differential equation.</li>
        <li>Simplify to obtain an equation involving <m>v</m>.</li>
        <li>Solve for <m>v</m>.</li>
        <li>Substitute back into <m>y_2=v(x)y_1(x)</m>.</li>
        <li>Write the general solution using both independent solutions.</li>
    </ol>

    <example xml:id="reduction-order-example">
        <title>Finding a Second Solution by Reduction of Order</title>

        <p>
            One solution of the differential equation
        </p>

        <me>
            y''-4y'+4y=0
        </me>

        <p>
            is
        </p>

        <me>
            y_1=e^{2x}.
        </me>

        <p>
            Use reduction of order to find a second linearly independent
            solution and write the general solution.
        </p>

        <solution>

            <p>
                Since one solution is already known, assume a second solution
                has the form
            </p>

            <me>
                y_2=v(x)y_1.
            </me>

            <p>
                Substituting <m>y_1=e^{2x}</m> gives
            </p>

            <me>
                y_2=v(x)e^{2x}.
            </me>

            <p>
                Compute the derivatives.
            </p>

            <me>
                y_2'
                =
                v'e^{2x}
                +
                2ve^{2x}
            </me>

            <me>
                y_2''
                =
                v''e^{2x}
                +
                4v'e^{2x}
                +
                4ve^{2x}.
            </me>

            <p>
                Substitute into the differential equation.
            </p>

            <me>
                \left(v''e^{2x}+4v'e^{2x}+4ve^{2x}\right)
                -
                4\left(v'e^{2x}+2ve^{2x}\right)
                +
                4ve^{2x}
                =
                0.
            </me>

            <p>
                Simplifying,
            </p>

            <me>
                v''e^{2x}=0.
            </me>

            <p>
                Since <m>e^{2x}\neq0</m>,
            </p>

            <me>
                v''=0.
            </me>

            <p>
                Integrate twice.
            </p>

            <me>
                v'=C_1
            </me>

            <me>
                v=C_1x+C_2.
            </me>

            <p>
                Therefore,
            </p>

            <me>
                y_2=(C_1x+C_2)e^{2x}.
            </me>

            <p>
                The term <m>C_2e^{2x}</m> reproduces the known solution
                <m>y_1</m>, so it does not provide a new independent solution.
            </p>

            <p>
                Choosing <m>C_1=1</m> and <m>C_2=0</m> gives
            </p>

            <me>
                y_2=xe^{2x}.
            </me>

            <p>
                Therefore, a fundamental set of solutions is
            </p>

            <me>
                y_1=e^{2x},
                \qquad
                y_2=xe^{2x}.
            </me>

            <p>
                The general solution is
            </p>

            <me>
                y=C_1e^{2x}+C_2xe^{2x}.
            </me>

        </solution>

    </example>

</subsection>
</section>
